## Decisions

- **The registry shrink is the contract change; the helper code is
  untouched.** The `for (const cmd of COMMANDS) { ... }` body in
  `scaffoldCommands` iterates whatever `COMMANDS` declares. Removing
  one entry from the array propagates through every consumer
  (planning, applying, upgrade-drift detection, info reporting)
  with zero algorithmic change. This matches the existing JSDoc
  contract: "Owning this list here — rather than discovering it
  from .claude/commands/ at runtime — is the mechanical guarantee
  that a stray Markdown file committed under .claude/commands/aide/
  cannot silently expand the pipeline."

- **Both registries (COMMANDS and DOC_PATHS) move together.**
  `scaffoldCommands.COMMANDS` references canonical names that must
  exist as keys in `initContent.DOC_PATHS`. Removing the canonical
  name from `DOC_PATHS` first lets TypeScript's
  `CanonicalDocName = keyof typeof DOC_PATHS` propagate the type
  shrink, which immediately surfaces any stale `COMMANDS` entry as
  a type error. That's the desired enforcement mechanism. The
  alternative (removing from `COMMANDS` first) leaves a valid type
  union that still includes a literal pointing at a deleted file —
  the lookup throws at runtime, not at compile time.

- **Tests assert against `COMMANDS.length`, not against a literal
  count.** The spec's `outcomes.undesired` line "a spec, test,
  description, or assertion that pins a specific count of slash
  commands or enumerates the canonical command roster as part of
  the contract" forbids `expect(results).toHaveLength(12)` for the
  same reason it forbids `13` today. Importing `COMMANDS` from
  `./index.js` and asserting `expect(results).toHaveLength(COMMANDS.length)`
  delegates the count to the source of truth — a future addition
  or removal updates the assertion automatically. The
  array-equality assertion (the `expect(results.map(...)).toEqual([...])`
  list) remains because the canonical ORDER is part of the
  contract this test protects (the displayed status report reads
  in COMMANDS-order and the user-facing tooling depends on it).
  Order is not a "count or enumeration" in the spec's sense — it
  is a structural invariant.

- **The `.aide` body rewrite is the implementor's responsibility,
  not the strategist's.** The user's brief explicitly delegated the
  body-section count-removal to the implementor as a "routine prose
  update, not a strategy change." The plan provides the rewrite
  rules (drop counts, drop enumerations, preserve reasoning); the
  implementor applies them. If any rewrite surfaces a true
  strategy question (e.g. should the `## Strategy` section name a
  specific lifecycle command at all?), escalate back to
  `/aide:spec`, not `/aide:plan` — that is a spec-rewrite question
  for `aide-spec-writer`, not an architecture decision.

- **The orphan `init.md` in existing host projects is NOT this
  plan's concern.** `scaffoldCommands` is a planner: it reports on
  registry entries, not on files in the host's command directory
  that aren't in the registry. After the registry shrinks, an
  existing host project's orphan `init.md` is invisible to
  planning — the file still works as a Claude Code slash command
  until the user runs `/aide:upgrade` or deletes it manually. The
  upgrade tool's deletion-handling is a separate scope; flagged in
  the sibling plan's Risks section.

- **Helper location preserved per user's deferred-refactor
  decision.** The `scaffoldCommands` module currently lives under
  `src/tools/init/`. The user has explicitly deferred a
  services-layer refactor that would lift this and its sibling
  helpers into a shared services package consumed by both the MCP
  tool and the cli. This plan does NOT preemptively touch that
  structure — the deletion-and-shrink stays minimal and contained
  inside the existing module location.

- **No new categories added to `aide_init`.** Per the user's
  brief, this is purely deletion + reference cleanup. The
  `commands` category (which `scaffoldCommands` produces steps
  for) shrinks by one entry; no new category is added. If during
  implementation a hidden coupling surfaces that suggests a new
  category is needed, STOP and surface it — do not add it
  unilaterally.

## Risks

- **The `aide_info` outdated-artifacts check may surface
  `commands/aide/init` as an "outdated" artifact for host projects
  that still have the file on disk.** `aide_info` compares the
  host's installed-artifact manifest (`.aide/versions.json`)
  against the canonical manifest shipped with the npm package.
  After this plan lands, the canonical manifest no longer lists
  `init.md`; the host's manifest still does. Whether this
  registers as `outdated` depends on the comparison semantics
  (does it flag artifacts only-on-host, or only artifacts where
  `sourceCommit` differs?). Confirm during build that the
  outdated check does not produce a confusing notification for
  host projects that haven't yet upgraded — if it does, that is a
  follow-up plan for `src/tools/info/buildVersionsState/` (or
  wherever the comparison happens).

- **Upgrade tool may not delete orphaned scaffolded files.** The
  `/aide:upgrade` command is documented as handling drift in
  shipped artifacts. Whether "drift" includes "artifact removed
  from the canonical set" is an open question — the upgrade flow
  may only handle add/update, not removal. If so, host projects
  will keep an orphan `.claude/commands/aide/init.md` until the
  user deletes it manually. The orphan is harmless (it is just a
  Markdown file Claude Code registers as a slash command), but it
  surfaces a user-facing inconsistency: the host's
  `/aide:init` still works, but its wizard prompt no longer
  reflects any tool the new server version exposes. Recommend
  testing upgrade in a smoke-test host project after publish to
  confirm deletion behavior; if upgrade only does add/update, a
  separate cleanup plan is needed for `src/tools/upgrade/`.

- **Test flakiness risk: switching from `toHaveLength(13)` to
  `toHaveLength(COMMANDS.length)` couples the test to the
  helper's own export.** This is intentional (the spec's
  no-count-pinning rule forces it), but it does mean a future bug
  that accidentally ships `COMMANDS` with the wrong shape would
  be invisible to this test — the assertion would still pass
  because the wrong shape would match its own length. The
  array-equality assertion in the cold-run test is the
  compensating control: it pins the canonical order and
  membership explicitly, so any accidental shape change would
  surface there. Watch for this in code review — a test reviewer
  should look for the array-equality assertion as the real
  contract, with `COMMANDS.length` as a secondary sanity check.

- **The `## Strategy` rewrite may produce drier prose than the
  current version.** The current text's enumeration of pipeline
  phases and lifecycle commands made the strategy concrete and
  readable; the count-free rewrite trades concreteness for
  drift-resistance. If the rewritten prose feels too abstract,
  the implementor may consider naming a small number of
  representative commands (e.g. "the seven pipeline phases" with
  no list, or "phase commands like research, plan, and qa" as
  illustrative not exhaustive) — but only if the resulting prose
  still passes the no-enumeration test. If the implementor isn't
  sure where the line is, escalate back to `/aide:spec` rather
  than improvising.
